PegResponsiveExponentialIrm::targetBorrowRate(uint256)
├── given price is in the healthy region
│   ├── when price equals recoveryPrice
│   │   └── it should return baseRate exactly
│   ├── when price is greater than recoveryPrice
│   │   └── it should return baseRate exactly
│   └── when price is type(uint256).max
│       └── it should return baseRate without performing subtractive curve math
├── given price is in the linear bridge where stressPrice <= price < recoveryPrice
│   ├── when price equals stressPrice
│   │   └── it should return stressRate exactly
│   ├── when price is one unit below recoveryPrice
│   │   └── it should apply the two-stage floor-rounded interpolation
│   ├── when price is one unit above stressPrice
│   │   └── it should apply the two-stage floor-rounded interpolation
│   ├── when price is an exact midpoint
│   │   └── it should return the corresponding midpoint rate when both divisions are exact
│   ├── when the bridge position is fractional
│   │   ├── it should first floor (recoveryPrice - price) times WAD divided by bridge width
│   │   └── it should then floor rateRange times the floored position divided by WAD
│   ├── when the two-stage calculation differs from a single-fraction interpolation by one rate unit
│   │   └── it should return the two-stage implementation result
│   ├── when recoveryPrice is exactly one unit above stressPrice
│   │   └── it should handle the minimum-width valid bridge without division by zero
│   └── when baseRate equals stressRate
│       └── it should return the same flat rate throughout the bridge
├── given price is below stressPrice
│   ├── it should calculate stressDrop as stressPrice minus price
│   ├── it should calculate rawExponent as floor(LN_2_WAD times stressDrop divided by doublingDistance)
│   ├── when rawExponent is less than MAX_EXPONENT_INPUT
│   │   └── it should pass rawExponent unchanged to ExpLib.wExp
│   ├── when rawExponent equals MAX_EXPONENT_INPUT
│   │   └── it should use MAX_EXPONENT_INPUT
│   ├── when rawExponent is greater than MAX_EXPONENT_INPUT
│   │   └── it should clamp the ExpLib input to MAX_EXPONENT_INPUT
│   ├── when rawExponent floors to zero
│   │   └── it should return stressRate unless a lower impossible cap is introduced outside validated configuration
│   ├── when rawExponent is non-zero but final fixed-point multiplication still rounds down to stressRate
│   │   └── it should return stressRate
│   ├── when stressDrop is one exact doublingDistance and the cap does not bind
│   │   └── it should return two times stressRate
│   ├── when stressDrop is two exact doubling distances and the cap does not bind
│   │   └── it should return four times stressRate
│   ├── when stressDrop is a fractional doubling distance and the cap does not bind
│   │   └── it should return the floor-rounded ExpLib approximation rather than require an exact mathematical square root
│   ├── when the uncapped rate is less than maxRate
│   │   └── it should return the uncapped rate
│   ├── when the uncapped rate equals maxRate
│   │   └── it should return maxRate exactly
│   ├── when the uncapped rate is greater than maxRate
│   │   └── it should return maxRate
│   ├── when price is one unit below stressPrice
│   │   └── it should return the exact exponential implementation result, which may equal or exceed stressRate
│   └── when price is zero and the depeg is extreme
│       ├── it should return no more than maxRate
│       └── it should not overflow for any constructor-valid configuration
├── given an arbitrary constructor-valid configuration and arbitrary price
│   ├── it should return a value greater than or equal to baseRate
│   ├── it should return a value less than or equal to maxRate
│   ├── when price is below stressPrice
│   │   └── it should return a value greater than or equal to stressRate
│   ├── when price decreases or stays equal
│   │   └── the raw target rate should increase or stay equal
│   ├── the piecewise formulas should meet at the exact recoveryPrice and stressPrice endpoints while adjacent integer inputs follow implementation rounding
│   └── it should not revert from width division, doubling-distance division, casts, or overflow
└── given any successful call
    ├── it should apply only the raw curve and not the stress-mode hysteresis floor
    ├── it should not require market binding or caller authorization
    ├── it should not read the oracle or call the market
    └── it should leave all state unchanged and emit no events
